# TuningFork, the Sonic Pi way

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tuning_fork, "~> 0.1.4"},
  {:tuning_fork_samples, "~> 0.1.4"},
  {:tuning_fork_kino, "~> 0.1.4"}
])
```

## The stage

`use TuningFork.SonicPi` brings in `play`, `sleep`, `sample`, `live_loop`, `with_fx` and the
rest. The stage below is what they play on; press **▶ listen** on it once, then evaluate any
loop cell. Evaluating a loop again swaps it in when it next comes round; `hush()` stops
everything.

```elixir
use TuningFork.SonicPi
```

```elixir
KinoTuningFork.stage()
```

```elixir
live_loop :bikes do
  with_synth :dsaw do
    with_fx :slicer, phase: choose([0.25, 0.125]) do
      with_fx :reverb, room: 0.5, mix: 0.3 do
        start_note = choose(chord(choose([:b1, :b2, :e1, :e2, :b3, :e3]), :minor))
        final_note = choose(chord(choose([:b1, :b2, :e1, :e2, :b3, :e3]), :minor))

        p = play start_note, release: 8, note_slide: 4, cutoff: 30, cutoff_slide: 4, detune: rrand(0, 0.2), pan: rrand(-1, 0), pan_slide: rrand(4, 8)
        control p, note: final_note, cutoff: rrand(80, 120), pan: rrand(0, 1)
      end
    end
  end

  sleep 8
end
```

```elixir
live_loop :bells do
  sample :perc_bell, rate: rrand(0.125, 1.5)
  sleep rrand(0, 2)
end
```

```elixir
play :e3
sleep 0.5
play :g3
```

